* collection_date:
June 2022 - Feb 2023

* description: 
This dataset was compiled as part of a PhD thesis chapter investigating how trait-mediated competition among tropical tree species is shaped by forest vertical structure. Leaf and wood economic traits were measured for 20 abundant tree species at the Bukit Timah Forest Dynamics Plot in Singapore and integrated with long-term forest census data. The central aim was to examine whether the same functional trait differences between competing species promote niche differentiation or competitive hierarchies depending on where those species sit along the vertical gradient, using maximum tree height as an axis of vertical niche and life-history strategy.

* sampling_strategy:
Traits were sampled for the 20 most stem-abundant tree species at the Bukit Timah Forest Dynamics Plot, collectively representing approximately 52% of all stems. For each species, 2–5 mature individuals were sampled within Bukit Timah Nature Reserve. Individuals were selected to represent typical adult size classes, restricted to those with diameter at breast height (DBH) above the 60th percentile of conspecific DBH within the plot, and individuals showing signs of herbivory, disease, or recent physical disturbance were excluded. Sun-exposed leaves were collected using a pole pruner (or shaded leaves for known understory species), with 5–8 leaves sampled per individual to account for within-crown variation. A single branch sample (1.5–4 cm diameter) was collected from individuals (where available) for wood density measurements.

* traits: 
* leaf dry matter content (lamina_ldmc, full_ldmc)
* mg/g
* individual
* mean
* >=5
* Leaves were rehydrated by wrapping leaves with soaked paper towels and left overnight at 4°C prior to measurement. For compound leaves, we measured individual leaflets, but pooled measurements together to obtain leaf-level data. Fresh leaf mass after rehydration was recorded immediately after removal from rehydration bags. Leaves were then oven-dried at 70°C for at least 1 week until reaching constant mass, and dry mass was recorded to the nearest 0.001 g using an analytical balance. LDMC was calculated as oven-dried leaf mass divided by fresh saturated mass. For each leaf, we calculated the LDMC of just the lamina (lamina_ldmc), as well as of the full leaf including the petiole (full_ldmc). Values represent means for each individual across all leaf samples. 

* specific leaf area (lamina_sla, full_sla)
* cm^2/g
* individual
* mean
* >=5
* Leaves were rehydrated by wrapping leaves with soaked paper towels and left overnight at 4°C prior to measurement. For compound leaves, we measured individual leaflets, but pooled measurements together to obtain leaf-level data. Rehydrated leaves were flattened and photographed against a light box to obtain clear silhouettes, and leaf area was quantified from these images using ImageJ. Leaves were then oven-dried at 70°C for at least 1 week until reaching constant mass, and dry mass was recorded to the nearest 0.001 g using an analytical balance. SLA was calculated as fresh leaf area divided by oven-dried leaf mass. For each leaf, we calculated the SLA of just the lamina (lamina_sla), as well as of the full leaf including the petiole (full_sla). Values represent means for each individual across all leaf samples. 

